
A linked list is a data structure in which elements (called nodes) are stored in different places in memory, and each node stores the address (pointer) of the next node. Unlike an array, it does not need continuous memory.

Structure of a node

Each node has two parts:

Data: the value to be stored
Next: a pointer to the next node
[10 | •]──►[20 | •]──►[30 | •]──►NULL
 head

The first node is called the head. The last node’s next is NULL, which marks the end of the list.

Simple C++ example

cpp
struct Node {
    int data;
    Node* next;
};

Node* head = new Node{10, nullptr};
head->next = new Node{20, nullptr};
head->next->next = new Node{30, nullptr};

Types of linked lists

Singly linked list: each node points only to the next node.
Doubly linked list: each node has both next and prev pointers, so you can traverse in both directions.
Circular linked list: the last node points back to the head.

Array vs Linked list

	Array	Linked List
Memory	Continuous	Scattered
Size	Fixed (usually)	Dynamic, grows/shrinks easily
Access by index	O(1)	O(n)
Insert/Delete (beginning)	O(n)	O(1)
Extra memory	None	Needed for pointers

Advantages

Insertion and deletion are easy, since elements don’t need to be shifted.
Size can change at runtime.

Disadvantages

No random access. To reach the 5th element, you have to walk 5 steps from the head.
Pointers use extra memory.
Cache performance is worse than arrays.

Basic operations

Traversal: start at the head and move until NULL
Insertion: at the beginning, at the end, or at a given position
Deletion: remove a node and reconnect the pointers
Search: check each node one by one

Insert at beginning (example)

cpp
void insertAtHead(Node*& head, int value) {
    Node* newNode = new Node{value, head};
    head = newNode;
}
